  <main>
    <h2>What a large language model is</h2>
    <p>A large language model is a very big neural network whose main job is to guess what text comes next. It is
      trained on a huge amount of writing, and from that it learns how words usually follow each other.</p>

    <figure>
      <img src="/img/transformer-architecture.png" alt="Diagram of the transformer design with encoder and decoder">
      <figcaption>The transformer design, with the encoder on the left and the decoder on the right</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <details>
      <summary>Do all language models use both parts of this diagram?</summary>
      <p>No. The diagram shows the original encoder-decoder transformer. Some language models use only the
        decoder portion to predict the next token.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>Text becomes tokens</h2>
    <p>The model cannot read letters. Text is first cut into tokens, which are usually a word or a piece of a word,
      and every token is turned into a number. A normal sentence might become eight to twelve tokens. This is why
      these models are measured in tokens instead of words.</p>

    <h2>Transformers and attention</h2>
    <p>The design behind modern language models is called a transformer. Its main idea is attention. While it
      works on one token, the model checks how important every other token in the passage is to that one. That is
      how it keeps track of what a sentence is really about.</p>

    <h2>Training and fine tuning</h2>
    <p>Pre-training happens once and costs the most. The model reads an enormous amount of text and keeps
      practicing the next word guess. Nothing has to be labelled, because the text itself gives the answer.</p>

    <p>Fine tuning happens afterwards and is much smaller. The trained model gets a little more training on a
      narrow set of examples so it follows instructions or works on one subject.</p>

